To determine the number of distinct triangles \(OPQ\) where \(O\) is the origin and \(P\) and \(Q\) are distinct points on the line \(41x + y = 2009\) with nonnegative integer coordinates, and whose area is a positive integer, we start by finding the possible coordinates of points \(P\) and \(Q\).

First, we find the range of \(x\) for which \(y = 2009 - 41x \geq 0\):
\[
41x \leq 2009 \implies x \leq \left\lfloor \frac{2009}{41} \right\rfloor = 49.
\]
Thus, \(x\) can take any integer value from 0 to 49. For each \(x\) in this range, the corresponding \(y\) is \(2009 - 41x\), so the points \(P\) and \(Q\) are \((x_1, 2009 - 41x_1)\) and \((x_2, 2009 - 41x_2)\) respectively, with \(0 \leq x_1 < x_2 \leq 49\).

The area of triangle \(OPQ\) is given by:
\[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \left| x_1(2009 - 41x_2) - x_2(2009 - 41x_1) \right| = \frac{1}{2} \left| 2009x_1 - 41x_1x_2 - 2009x_2 + 41x_2x_1 \right| = \frac{1}{2} \left| 2009(x_1 - x_2) \right| = \frac{2009}{2} \left| x_1 - x_2 \right|.
\]
For the area to be a positive integer, \(\left| x_1 - x_2 \right|\) must be an even number because 2009 is an odd number (odd * even = even). Therefore, \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) must have the same parity (both even or both odd).

