To determine the probability that the sum of four randomly selected prime numbers from the first ten prime numbers is odd, we first list the first ten prime numbers. They are: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, and 29.

Notice that among these, there is only one even prime number, which is 2. The rest of the prime numbers are odd. The sum of four numbers is odd if and only if exactly one of the numbers is even (since the sum of an even number of odd numbers is even, and the sum of an odd number of odd numbers is odd).

Therefore, for the sum of four selected numbers to be odd, we must select exactly one even number and three odd numbers. In this case, the only even number available is 2, so we must select 2 and any three of the remaining nine odd prime numbers.

The number of ways to select 3 odd numbers from 9 is given by the combination formula \( \binom{9}{3} \):
\[
\binom{9}{3} = \frac{9!}{3!6!} = \frac{9 \times 8 \times 7}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 84
\]

