The largest inscribed triangle with one side as the diameter will have the other two vertices such that the triangle is a right triangle with the diameter as the hypotenuse. The height of this triangle will be the radius of the circle, which is 6 cm, because the height is the perpendicular distance from the center of the circle (the midpoint of the diameter) to the arc, which is the radius.

The area \(A\) of a triangle is given by the formula:
\[
A = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
\]
