The smallest three-digit palindromic prime is \( 101 \). The next palindromic number is \( 111 \). However, \( 111 = 3 \times 37 \), so it is not prime. The next palindromic number is \( 121 \). However, \( 121 = 11 \times 11 \), so it is not prime. The next palindromic number is \( 131 \). To check if \( 131 \) is prime, we test divisibility by all prime numbers less than \( \sqrt{131} \approx 11.45 \), which are \( 2, 3, 5, 7, \) and \( 11 \).

- \( 131 \) is not even, so it is not divisible by \( 2 \).
- The sum of the digits of \( 131 \) is \( 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 \), which is divisible by \( 5 \), so it is not divisible by \( 11 \).

Therefore, \( 151 \) is not divisible by any of these prime numbers, so \( 151 \) is prime.

Thus.

