To determine the probability that team \( A \) finishes with more points than team \( B \) given that team \( A \) wins their head-to-head match, we need to consider the outcomes of the remaining games each team plays.

Each team plays 6 games in total, with team \( A \) already having won 1 game against team \( B \). Therefore, team \( A \) has 5 games left to play, and team \( B \) also has 5 games left to play.

Let \( X \) be the number of games team \( A \) wins out of its remaining 5 games, and \( Y \) be the number of games team \( B \) wins out of its remaining 5 games. Both \( X \) and \( Y \) follow a binomial distribution with parameters \( n = 5 \) and \( p = 0.5 \).

 Team \( A \)'s total points will be \( 1 + X \) (since it has already won 1 game), and team \( B \)'s total points will be \( Y \). We need to find the probability that team \( A \)'s total points are greater than team \( B \)'s total points, i.e., the probability that \( 1 + X > Y \).

This is equivalent to finding the probability that \( X \geq Y \). Since \( X \) and \( Y \) are identically distributed, we know that:

\[
P(X > Y) = P(Y > X) \quad \text{and} \quad P(X = Y) = \binom{5}{0} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^5 + \binom{5}{1} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^5 + \binom{5}{2} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^5 + \binom{5}{3} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^5 + \binom{3}{4} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^5 + -\binom'
